N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/3 (last sentence) + [dcl.init.general]/9: for
libstdc++'s `__can_owning_view<int(&)[5]>` (`owning_view{declval<_Range>()}`)
the constructor-derived guide `owning_view(_Range&&)` deduced `_Range =
int(&)[5]` by the forwarding-reference rule, the class's `_Range _M_r =
_Range()` then value-initialised a reference and cpp_constructor aborted on
its not-a-reference invariant (the whole run dumped core).  Now `_Range =
int[5]`, `movable<int[5]>` is false and the requirement is simply
unsatisfied; a reference value-initialisation is an ordinary error.
Non-vacuous: the last assertion states a wrong value and must FAIL.
